          </div>

          <div class="spec-rules">
            <div class="spec-rule spec-rule--red"><span>Rule 01</span><h3>No <code>null</code>.</h3><p>An unknown fact is explicit and typed:</p><pre><code>&#123;<span class="key">"unknown"</span>: <span class="boolean">true</span>,
 <span class="key">"reason"</span>: <span class="string">"not_recorded"</span>,
 <span class="key">"detail"</span>: <span class="string">"provider did not attest model"</span>&#125;</code></pre></div>
            <div class="spec-rule"><span>Rule 02</span><h3>Subject ≠ context.</h3><p>System + task identify what the number is about. Configuration + environment identify the conditions. Each reduces to its own canonical digest.</p></div>
            <div class="spec-rule"><span>Rule 03</span><h3>Every attempt survives.</h3><p>Passed, failed, error, timeout, aborted, not-run, and unknown attempts remain ordered with counted, discarded, or superseded disposition.</p></div>
            <div class="spec-rule"><span>Rule 04</span><h3>Extensions cannot change the verdict.</h3><p>Namespaced extensions are sealed as bytes but excluded from subject/context digests, selection, coverage, and the core receipt diff.</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="spec-bundle">
          <div class="spec-bundle__tree">
            <span>Portable bundle</span>
            <code>evaluation.proofpack/</code>
            <code>├── <b>proof.lock</b> <i>inventory + root binding</i></code>
            <code>├── <b>evidence.jsonl</b> <i>chained sealing log</i></code>
            <code>├── <b>receipt.json</b> <i>one evaluated cell</i></code>
            <code>├── attempts/1/<b>transcript.json</b></code>
            <code>├── attempts/1/<b>patch.diff</b></code>
            <code>├── attempts/1/<b>build.txt</b></code>
            <code>└── attempts/1/<b>browser-effects.json</b></code>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-outcomes">
            <span>Verifier outcomes</span>
            <div><b>verified_full</b><p>Every locked artifact is present and matches; receipt, evidence chain, inventory, root, and subject/context bindings agree.</p></div>
            <div><b>verified_detached</b><p><code>proof.lock</code> and <code>receipt.json</code> agree, but absent locked artifacts stay explicitly unconfirmed.</p></div>
            <div><b>invalid</b><p>A typed code identifies the first failed rule. Invalid never degrades into detached or unresolved success.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-limit">
            <span>What verification still does not prove</span>
            <p>That the evidence is true, complete, independently authored, produced under the claimed process, or that the evaluator measures the owner’s intended meaning. The spec makes those questions inspectable; it does not answer them by assertion.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <aside class="spec-conformance">
          <div><span>Normative</span><strong>Markdown specification text</strong><small>Wins where JSON Schema cannot express kind, ordering, or cross-member rules.</small></div>
          <div><span>Informative</span><strong>JSON Schemas</strong><small>Useful for tooling, but not the final authority over canonical integers/reals and validation order.</small></div>
          <div><span>Executable</span><strong>Conformance corpora</strong><small>Receipt corpus: 111 cases—58 validate, 15 verify, 30 diff, and 8 build.</small></div>
          <div><span>Independent</span><strong>TypeScript + Python; Go subset</strong><small>Implementations speak one subprocess protocol; a static browser verifier handles evidence bundles.</small></div>
        </aside>

        <div class="spec-commands">
          <div><span>Use the spec repository</span><h3>One command surface for every implementation.</h3><p>The corpus—not a prose claim—decides whether another language agrees.</p></div>
          <pre><code><span class="comment"># From the local lab-specs repository</span>
cd /Users/alvarostudio/Code/lab-specs
make verify

<span class="comment"># Drive independent implementations over the same corpus.</span>
python3 harness/run_conformance.py \
  --impl <span class="string">"node impl/ts/dist/cli.js"</span>
python3 harness/run_conformance.py \
  --impl <span class="string">".venv/bin/python impl/py/adapter.py"</span>
python3 harness/run_conformance.py \
  --impl <span class="string">"impl/go/evalreceipt-verify"</span> --spec canonical evidence

<span class="comment"># Create a self-contained implementation packet.</span>
python3 harness/make_packet.py \
  --spec evidence --language go --out packets/</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="mapping-note"><span>Historical mapping revision 2</span><p>New agent-eval receipts preserve retained historical facts in the core and keep unverifiable current assumptions unknown. Current-source digests, reconstructed parameters, and build-time observations live under a namespaced extension; they may change the sealed document without pretending they were observed in the original run.</p></div>

        <div class="spec-not-pack"><b>The important boundary:</b> a Workbench pack is an executable evaluation definition. A lab-spec receipt is a portable account of one measured cell. The pack can produce many receipts; a receipt cannot silently redefine the pack.</div>
      </section>

      <section class="ew-section" id="walkthrough">
        <div class="section-intro">
          <div>
            <p class="section-code">05 / What works today</p>
            <h2>The synthetic supplier review,<br />read honestly.</h2>
          </div>
          <p>The current example is deliberately fictional. It is valuable because it makes the mechanics inspectable without provider spend—not because it represents customer validation.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="walkthrough-layout">
          <div class="terminal-card">
            <div class="terminal-card__bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span><b>recorded / zero-provider</b></div>
            <pre><code><span class="cmd">$</span> evalworkbench init demo-project --example
<span class="cmd">$</span> cd demo-project

<span class="comment"># Challenge the meaning before trusting the evaluator.</span>
<span class="cmd">$</span> evalworkbench challenge-meaning \
    --case beta-ready --reviewer control-owner

correct_control           <span class="pass">PASS</span>
wrong_authority           <span class="fail">FAIL</span>
invented_citation         <span class="fail">FAIL</span>
unsupported_claim         <span class="unknown">UNRESOLVED</span>
missing_approval          <span class="unknown">UNRESOLVED</span>

<span class="comment"># Replay retained Beta bytes. No agent is dispatched.</span>
<span class="cmd">$</span> evalworkbench run \
    --case beta-ready --mode recorded --json run.json

lifecycle                 CLOSED
verdict                   <span class="pass">PASS</span>
checks                    8 / 8
provider_calls            0</code></pre>
          </div>

          <div class="verdict-card">
            <div class="verdict-card__head"><span class="state state--current">Imported evidence</span><span>Beta / recorded</span></div>
            <div class="verdict-score"><strong>8</strong><span>/ 8 checks</span></div>
            <h3>Pass—with a narrow claim.</h3>
            <p>The retained output has citations; supplier scope, authority, version, and expiry hold; required criteria are complete; conflicts are accounted for; and the saved artifact is structurally valid.</p>
            <div class="not-run"><b>No agent ran.</b> The replay proves deterministic evaluation behavior over known bytes.</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="meaning-grid">
          <article><span class="result result--pass">Pass</span><h3>Evidence satisfies the reviewed check.</h3><p>Example: Beta cites current, authoritative supplier-scoped records for every required criterion.</p></article>
          <article><span class="result result--fail">Fail</span><h3>Evidence contradicts a qualified expectation.</h3><p>Example: an invented citation, wrong supplier, expired evidence, or open finding is observed.</p></article>
          <article><span class="result result--unsupported">Unsupported</span><h3>The requested claim is outside the check’s supported scope.</h3><p>Example: one approval citation is asserted as proof for every distinct criterion.</p></article>
          <article><span class="result result--unresolved">Unresolved</span><h3>A decision-bearing basis is missing.</h3><p>Example: Acme has a conditional approval with an open finding. It is neither silently passed nor forced into a semantic failure.</p></article>
        </div>

        <div class="manual-loop">
          <div class="manual-loop__title"><span>Current correction path</span><h3>Manual, explicit, history-preserving.</h3></div>
          <ol>
            <li><b>Inspect</b><span>Open the witness or missing basis behind the result.</span></li>
            <li><b>Classify</b><span>Agent/output defect, legitimate alternative, bad expectation, or missing observation?</span></li>
            <li><b>Revise</b><span>Change the agent, meaning, checker, or capture path under a new identity.</span></li>
            <li><b>Challenge</b><span>Run correct, failing, unsupported, and unresolved controls again.</span></li>
            <li><b>Authorize</b><span>Freeze the case and explicitly approve a fresh execution.</span></li>
            <li><b>Confirm + keep</b><span>Check the fix and regressions; retain old and new evidence.</span></li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="evidence-table-wrap">
          <table class="evidence-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Evidence kind</th><th>It can support</th><th>It cannot support</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {evidenceKinds.map(([kind, supports, not]) => <tr><th>{kind}</th><td>{supports}</td><td>{not}</td></tr>)}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
